1
00:00:13,800 --> 00:00:20,009
Open source is worth an estimated eight point eight
trillion dollars to the companies that use it.

2
00:00:20,059 --> 00:00:23,075
Rewriting it once would cost about four billion.

3
00:00:23,400 --> 00:00:27,005
Five percent of developers, roughly three thousand
people,

4
00:00:27,055 --> 00:00:29,525
create ninety-six percent of that value.

5
00:00:29,850 --> 00:00:34,803
When Heartbleed hit, OpenSSL ran on under two thousand
dollars a year.

6
00:00:34,853 --> 00:00:40,950
The xz backdoor was planted over two years, in a
project run by one unpaid maintainer.

7
00:00:41,300 --> 00:00:46,478
Now AI writes the code, and fewer people ever see the
docs or the sponsor button.

8
00:00:46,528 --> 00:00:52,675
Tailwind lost about forty percent of its docs traffic,
and nearly eighty percent of its revenue.

9
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:06,664
Why PayPal? Maintainers live everywhere.

10
00:01:06,714 --> 00:01:13,043
PayPal Payouts reach ninety-six markets in twenty-four
currencies, with just an email address.

11
00:01:13,093 --> 00:01:18,200
A subscription holds the human's mandate, and one
batch pays everyone, once.

12
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:20,302
Setup is two steps.

13
00:01:20,352 --> 00:01:25,283
Set a monthly budget, and one click redirects to
PayPal to approve it.

14
00:01:25,333 --> 00:01:29,125
That approval is the agent's mandate, and its ceiling.

15
00:01:29,600 --> 00:01:34,850
Then one hook line. Package names and depth only.
Never source code.

16
00:01:35,400 --> 00:01:39,363
At month end, the AI Treasurer answers typed
questions:

17
00:01:39,413 --> 00:01:44,252
how critical is each package, and how much do its
maintainers need?

18
00:01:44,302 --> 00:01:47,025
Code turns those answers into amounts.

19
00:01:47,500 --> 00:01:53,575
Same budget. Judgment moves this package from
sixty-nine dollars to one sixty-one.

20
00:01:54,300 --> 00:01:58,291
A security advisory? The AI can propose a grant.

21
00:01:58,341 --> 00:02:01,575
Only a human can approve it, on PayPal.

22
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:07,317
Month end. A poisoned README asks for the maximum
score.

23
00:02:07,367 --> 00:02:11,450
The answer is rejected, and a neutral weight is used.

24
00:02:11,900 --> 00:02:15,825
A recipient who just changed their payout email is
held.

25
00:02:16,200 --> 00:02:20,300
Seven invariants, checked in code, before a cent
moves.

26
00:02:20,800 --> 00:02:26,575
The AI decides where the money goes. The code decides
where it can never go.

27
00:02:28,500 --> 00:02:33,575
Maya, a fictional maintainer, sees every payment, and
exactly why.

28
00:02:38,900 --> 00:02:42,400
Run it twice. Same batch. No double pay.
